On Sun, 11 Mar 2007 09:13:02 +0100, guns <sungmustpae@gmail.com> wrote:

> I am applying various patches that I have found interesting (most of
> them L's patches: http://www.greyfire.org/~l/nethack.html ), but I am
> running into this error message:
>
> /usr/src/nethack$ patch -p1 --dry-run < poisoncloud.diff
> patching file src/zap.c
> Hunk #1 FAILED at 3670.
> 1 out of 1 hunk FAILED -- saving rejects to file src/zap.c.rej

This also happens a lot when you apply several patches in a row (or  
patches intended for other version).

A hunk is a basic piece of patche to be inserted at the right place in the  
right file. To know what is the right place, patch has two infos: 1/ the  
line number and 2/ the surrounding lines (usually something like 2 or 3  
above and below).

Sometimes, the line number is not correct (typically because the file was  
patched at previous lines and extra lines where added). In that case, the  
surrounding lines allow patch to find the right place (most of the time)  
and patch the file but producing a warning.

Sometimes, the surrounding lines are not correct. Typically because a  
previous patch, or a change in version, has modified them. At this point,  
patch has no way of knowing where to apply the hunk (the line number is  
useless since the lines to patch might have moved). So it produce the  
error message you've seen.
In that case, looking at the .rej file allow to have a precise idea of  
what failed as well as a clue of why it failed: the .rej file shows both  
the patch (ie what was expected and what should be added/removed/modified)  
and the stuff found at the given position. By comparing the expected stuff  
and the real stuff, you can see why patch failed.
However, I'm quite afraid that at this point you have to apply the failed  
hunks by hand (the other being already applied).

What I usually do is to delete the failed hunk from the .rej file one by  
one (sometimes there are many of them) after solving each problem.

